## What I kept or threw away, and why

### What I kept
AI generated the screens, but I deliberately stayed close to what already exists on the MatchHire website instead of designing something new. I kept the site's existing colors, fonts, layout, and navigation links so the redesign still feels like the same product. My goal was to fix one specific problem, not to rebrand the site.

- I kept "Must Hunt" as a link with its original name and purpose, since I don't know exactly what the platform relies on it for.
- I kept the existing content and structure of the Job Seeker and Employer screens.
- I kept the "How it works" section from the original job seeker page. The AI left it out of its first design, so I wrote a follow-up prompt to bring it back with the original steps and wording.

### What I threw away
I removed AI-generated elements that did not exist on the MatchHire website and did not help solve the problem:
- [Example 1: e.g., decorative illustrations or extra sections]
- [Example 2: e.g., additional links or features the AI invented]

I also changed my own earlier direction. My first prompts told the AI to rename "Must Hunt" to "Job Openings." I dropped that idea because renaming a link whose purpose I didn't fully understand was riskier than adding a new one.

### What I added
I added two things to the design, both aimed at the same problem.

**1. A clearer entry point.** I reworked the entry point compared to the existing website. The top navigation now has two clearly labeled paths, "Job Seeker" and "Employer," so a new visitor can tell right away which one is for them. On the existing site, the entry points were easy to confuse (entry for job seekers platformcalled "job search"), and that confusion is what sent new users down the wrong path.

**2. A separate "Job Openings" link.** I added a "Job Openings" link to the Job Seeker navigation, next to "Must Hunt." On the live site, open roles sit under "Must Hunt," a name that doesn't signal job openings.

I added these to solve the problem I identified: because of the confusing entry point and the unclear naming, new visitors couldn't find where to apply for jobs. In testing, 3 of 3 could not find open roles without help. Together, the two changes let a new job seeker reach open roles in 2 clicks (Job Seeker → Job Openings), with no account prompt.

### Why I made these choices
Both changes address the same chain: unclear entry point, then unclear naming (entry for job seekers platformcalled "job search"), then not finding roles. Everything else stayed close to the existing site, so any improvement in the 2-click test comes from these navigation changes and not from a different look. It also keeps the redesign small and realistic for the MatchHire team to build.
